 in glaucomatous optic neuropathy.</article-title><source>Arch Ophthalmol</source><year>1997</year><volume>115</volume><fpage>389</fpage><lpage>95</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9076213</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r6"><label>6</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Burgoyne</surname><given-names>CF</given-names></name><name><surname>Downs</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Bellezza</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Suh</surname><given-names>JK</given-names></name><name><surname>Hart</surname><given-names>RT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The optic nerve head as a biomechanical structure: a new paradigm for understanding the role of IOP-related stress and strain in the pathophysiology of glaucomatous optic nerve head damage.</article-title><source>Prog Retin Eye Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>39</fpage><lpage>73</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15555526</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r7"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Edwards</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Good</surname><given-names>TA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Use of a mathematical model to estimate stress and strain during elevated pressure induced lamina cribrosa deformation.</article-title><source>Curr Eye Res</source><year>2001</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>215</fpage><lpage>25</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11803484</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r8"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Quigley</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name><name><surname>Addicks</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Chronic experimental glaucoma in primates. II. Effect of extended intraocular pressure elevation on optic nerve hea